
Lunaria's AD Blocker

A lightweight Chromium extension that blocks unwanted ads using Manifest V3's
`declarativeNetRequest` ruleset. Primarily tested on Opera GX and other
Chromium-based browsers.

**Known issues**
- In some cases a video player (e.g. YouTube) may stop playing when an ad is
	removed; a page refresh may resolve this.

Project structure (top-level files)

- `manifest.json` — Extension manifest (Manifest V3).
- `background.js` — Service worker / background logic.
- `content.js` — Content script injected into pages at `document_start`.
- `blocker.css` — CSS injected by the content script to hide elements.
- `rules.json` — Declarative Net Request rules used for blocking.
- `popup.html` — UI shown when clicking the extension action icon.
- `icon16.png`, `icon48.png`, `icon128.png` — Extension icons.
- `_metadata/` — Generated ruleset index used when building the rules JSON.

How it works

- The extension uses Manifest V3 and `declarativeNetRequest` for network-level
	blocking via `rules.json`.
- A content script (`content.js`) runs at `document_start` and injects
	`blocker.css` and any DOM-manipulation logic needed to hide dynamic
	ad-related elements.
- `background.js` runs as a service worker and coordinates any background
	tasks and state stored via the `storage` permission.

Installation (Load unpacked)

1. Open your Chromium-based browser and go to `chrome://extensions/`.
2. Enable "Developer mode" (top-right).
3. Click "Load unpacked" and select this project's folder (the one
	 containing `manifest.json`).
4. The extension should appear in the toolbar.

Note: Packaging (.crx) and direct installation may be blocked by some browsers;
use the unpacked flow for development and testing.

Permissions used

- `declarativeNetRequest` — Apply blocking rules from `rules.json`.
- `storage` — Persist user preferences.
- `host_permissions` (`<all_urls>`) — Allow content scripts and rules to run on
	all sites.

Editing and regenerating rules

- The active ruleset is `rules.json`. A generated ruleset is stored under
	`_metadata/generated_indexed_rulesets/`. Update your source rules and
	regenerate `rules.json` using your rules-generation workflow.

Contributing

- Send pull requests for bug fixes or improvements. Keep changes small and
	focused.

